      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z"/><polyline points="14 2 14 8 20 8"/></svg>
      Context Fluency (2025)
    </a>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- THREE-COLUMN GRID -->
<div class="post-grid" id="post-grid">

<!-- LEFT: TOC -->
<nav class="toc-sidebar" id="toc-sidebar">
  <div class="toc-head">
    <span class="toc-label">Contents</span>
    <button class="toc-toggle" id="toc-toggle" title="Hide contents">‹</button>
    <span class="toc-collapsed-label">nav</span>
  </div>
  <ol class="toc-list">
    <li class="toc-item" id="toc-s-paradox"><a href="#s-paradox"><span class="toc-num">01</span> The Paradox</a></li>
    <li class="toc-item" id="toc-s-evidence"><a href="#s-evidence"><span class="toc-num">02</span> What Research Shows</a></li>
    <li class="toc-item" id="toc-s-patterns"><a href="#s-patterns"><span class="toc-num">03</span> Six Interaction Patterns</a></li>
    <li class="toc-item" id="toc-s-skills"><a href="#s-skills"><span class="toc-num">04</span> Skills That Matter</a></li>
    <li class="toc-item" id="toc-s-framework"><a href="#s-framework"><span class="toc-num">05</span> The Decision Framework</a></li>
    <li class="toc-item" id="toc-s-plan"><a href="#s-plan"><span class="toc-num">06</span> Your 90-Day Plan</a></li>
  </ol>
</nav>

<!-- MIDDLE: ARTICLE -->
<article class="post-body">

<!-- ── §01 ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-paradox" data-code="paradox">
  <div class="section-no">01 — The Paradox</div>
  <h2>You're Shipping Fast. But Are You Growing?</h2>

  <p>Every software engineer is using AI tools. The question isn't whether to use them — it's whether you're using them in a way that makes you stronger or weaker over time.</p>

  <p>Here's the paradox. A controlled experiment (Peng et al., 2023) found that developers using GitHub Copilot completed a JavaScript server task <strong>55.8% faster</strong> than a control group. That's a staggering productivity gain. But a separate 2025 study (arXiv:2601.20245) found that AI assistance <em>impairs</em> conceptual understanding, code reading, and debugging ability — with no significant efficiency gains on average — when users engage passively with the output.</p>

  <p>You can get the speed without the skill loss. But only if you understand why the loss happens and how to prevent it. The chart below shows two developer trajectories over 24 months starting today.</p>

  <div class="vp">
    <div class="vp-head">
      <span class="vp-title">developer trajectory — 24-month skill projection</span>
      <span class="vp-hint">click a time marker to see the snapshot</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-body">
      <canvas class="traj-canvas" id="traj-canvas" height="280"></canvas>
      <div class="traj-btns">
        <button class="traj-btn traj-active" data-month="0">Today</button>
        <button class="traj-btn" data-month="6">6 months</button>
        <button class="traj-btn" data-month="12">1 year</button>
        <button class="traj-btn" data-month="24">2 years</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-purple">
    <span class="callout-label">Key insight</span>
    At 6 months, the passive AI user looks more productive. By 12 months, the deliberate practitioner has overtaken them. By 2 years, the gap is decisive — and the passive user's skills have <em>declined</em> from where they started.
  </div>

  <p>The green path (Sam) and red path (Alex) represent the same person starting with the same skills, using the same AI tools, but with different engagement strategies. The difference isn't access — it's behavior. This post is about understanding that behavior difference and building the habits to stay on the green path.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── §02 ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-evidence" data-code="evidence">
  <div class="section-no">02 — What the Research Shows</div>
  <h2>The Data Behind the Divergence</h2>

  <p>This isn't speculation. Six research papers published between 2023 and 2025 converge on a consistent picture: AI tools create real short-term productivity gains, but passive use creates real long-term capability risks.</p>

  <div class="cite-list">
    <div class="cite-card">
      <span class="cite-arxiv">2302.06590</span>
      <div class="cite-body">
        <div class="cite-title">The Impact of AI on Developer Productivity (Peng et al., 2023)</div>
        <div class="cite-authors">Controlled experiment, N=95 developers. Task: build an HTTP server in JS. Result: +55.8% faster with Copilot.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cite-card">
      <span class="cite-arxiv">2601.20245</span>
      <div class="cite-body">
        <div class="cite-title">How AI Impacts Skill Formation (2025)</div>
        <div class="cite-authors">Passive AI use impairs conceptual understanding, code reading, and debugging. No significant efficiency gain when engagement is low. "AI-enhanced productivity is not a shortcut to competence."</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cite-card">
      <span class="cite-arxiv">2512.08942</span>
      <div class="cite-body">
        <div class="cite-title">Comprehension Debt: AI-Assisted Development in Indie Teams (2024)</div>
        <div class="cite-authors">3-person team, 3-month study, 157 Jira tasks, 333 commits. Teams built systems "more sophisticated than their independent skill level can maintain" — a new form of technical debt.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cite-card">
      <span class="cite-arxiv">2605.27399</span>
      <div class="cite-body">
        <div class="cite-title">Short-Term Gain, Long-Term Fragility (2025)</div>
        <div class="cite-authors">"Capability masking" — AI output creates the illusion that organizational capability has been replaced while skills are actually eroding. Skills are "slow to build and difficult to restore."</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cite-card">
      <span class="cite-arxiv">2512.23982</span>
      <div class="cite-body">
        <div class="cite-title">AI Coding in Industrial Practice (2024–25)</div>
        <div class="cite-authors">Analysis of 57 YouTube practitioner videos. Practitioners report "erosion of foundational problem-solving skills" and "insufficient preparation of entry-level engineers" alongside productivity gains.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cite-card">
      <span class="cite-arxiv">2605.05400</span>
      <div class="cite-body">
        <div class="cite-title">Mise en Place for Agentic Coding (2025)</div>
        <div class="cite-authors">Introduces "context fluency" — the emerging skill of creating rich structured context for AI agents. Deliberate preparation (2 hrs) enabled a full-stack platform to be built during a hackathon.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>The Two Types of Impact</h3>
  <p>There is a clean split: AI tools reliably improve task-level throughput. But the same tools, used passively, impair the deeper skills. The bar chart below shows this split. Note that "impaired" means research found statistically significant decline — not a specific percentage, which varies by study and context.</p>

