  <h3>The Emerging Skill: Context Fluency</h3>
  <p>arXiv:2605.05400 introduces a crucial new concept: <strong>context fluency</strong> — "the ability to create rich, structured context that agents can act on." As AI becomes more powerful at implementation, the developer's value increasingly lies in <em>specifying what to build</em> precisely and completely.</p>

  <div class="callout callout-green">
    <span class="callout-label">Context Fluency in Practice</span>
    The paper's team spent ~2 hours writing structured specs, design documents, and task decompositions before touching code. AI agents then implemented a full-stack platform in parallel. The 2 hours of human investment unlocked 10× leverage — but only because the humans had the architectural understanding to write good specs.
  </div>

  <p>Context fluency <em>compounds with deep understanding</em>. The developer who deeply understands distributed systems writes better specs for distributed systems. The developer who has debugged real race conditions writes better edge-case constraints. Shallow knowledge leads to shallow specs leads to shallow AI output.</p>

  <h3>What This Means Practically</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Code generation is a commodity.</strong> If your value proposition is "I write code fast," AI has mostly commoditized that. Junior developers with AI access can match a senior's raw throughput on standard tasks.</li>
    <li><strong>Code review is now the primary bottleneck.</strong> arXiv:2512.23982 found practitioners consistently reporting code review as the new constraint. Reviewing AI-generated code requires deeper understanding than reviewing human code — because AI makes plausible-looking errors in unfamiliar ways.</li>
    <li><strong>System design is the widening moat.</strong> AI can generate implementations from specs. It cannot yet reliably generate the <em>right</em> specs from business requirements. The human who can go from "we need to handle 100K concurrent users" to a coherent distributed architecture — that skill is growing in value.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- ── §05 ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-framework" data-code="framework">
  <div class="section-no">05 — The Decision Framework</div>
  <h2>Choosing How to Engage: The 2×2 Matrix</h2>

  <p>For any task, two variables determine the right AI engagement strategy:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Task complexity</strong> — is this a well-understood, bounded problem (simple) or a design problem with many unknowns (complex)?</li>
    <li><strong>Your current mastery</strong> — do you have a deep mental model of this domain, or are you still building one?</li>
  </ul>

  <p>These two axes create four zones, each with a different optimal strategy. Click any zone to see the recommended approach.</p>

  <div class="vp">
    <div class="vp-head">
      <span class="vp-title">ai engagement decision matrix — click any zone</span>
      <span class="vp-hint">click to explore strategies</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-body">
      <canvas class="matrix-canvas" id="matrix-canvas" height="340"></canvas>
      <div class="matrix-desc" id="matrix-desc">
        <div class="matrix-zone-name" id="matrix-zone-name" style="color:var(--muted)">Click a zone →</div>
        <div id="matrix-zone-body">Select a quadrant above to see the recommended strategy for tasks in that zone.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout-purple">
    <span class="callout-label">The core principle</span>
    The framework isn't about limiting AI use — it's about calibrating <em>your cognitive engagement</em>. In the Study and Dissect zones, you must stay mentally active while AI helps. In the Delegation zone, you can genuinely delegate because you already have the knowledge to supervise. The Collaboration zone is where senior engineers operate at their best.
  </div>

  <h3>Applying the Framework Day-to-Day</h3>
  <p>When you start a task, ask: <em>"Am I in a zone where I should engage, or a zone where I can delegate?"</em> The goal is to spend time in the Study and Dissect zones early in your career, move tasks progressively to Delegation as you master them, and reserve your energy for Collaboration on the high-value complex problems.</p>

  <p>A practical rule of thumb: if you couldn't explain the AI's output to a colleague in plain English, you're not ready to delegate that task yet. Use it as a Study Zone session instead.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── §06 ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-plan" data-code="plan">
  <div class="section-no">06 — Your 90-Day Plan</div>
  <h2>Starting From Today</h2>

  <p>The trajectory in §01 is not predetermined. You can change paths at any point. The most important thing is to start with concrete, specific habits — not vague intentions to "be more intentional." Here's a structured 90-day plan based on the research.</p>

  <div class="phase-grid">
    <div class="phase-card">
      <div class="phase-hdr">
        <div class="phase-num">1</div>
        <div class="phase-title">Audit Your AI Usage</div>
      </div>
      <div class="phase-date">Days 1–14</div>
      <ul class="phase-items">
        <li>For one week, log every AI suggestion you accept without reading it</li>
        <li>Identify the 3 task types you most frequently copy-paste blindly</li>
        <li>Pick one of those 3 tasks and commit to reading every line for the next week</li>
        <li>Ask: "Can I explain this code to a colleague right now?"</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="phase-card">
      <div class="phase-hdr">
        <div class="phase-num">2</div>
        <div class="phase-title">Deliberate Slowdown</div>
      </div>
      <div class="phase-date">Days 15–30</div>
      <ul class="phase-items">
        <li>Switch your 3 identified blind-delegation tasks to Guided Generation mode</li>
        <li>For each AI suggestion: read it, modify at least one thing, verify you understand why</li>
        <li>When you hit a bug, try diagnosing before asking AI — even if it takes 10 more minutes</li>
        <li>Use AI as a tutor: ask it to explain concepts, not just give answers</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="phase-card">
      <div class="phase-hdr">
        <div class="phase-num">3</div>
        <div class="phase-title">Build the Understanding Layer</div>
      </div>
      <div class="phase-date">Days 31–60</div>
      <ul class="phase-items">
        <li>Write a design doc from memory for one system you currently own</li>
        <li>Read through AI-generated tests and verify you could write them without AI</li>
        <li>Practice explaining your codebase to an imaginary new teammate</li>
        <li>Find one piece of code you accepted months ago that you can't fully explain — dissect it now</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="phase-card">
      <div class="phase-hdr">
        <div class="phase-num">4</div>
        <div class="phase-title">Develop the New Skills</div>
      </div>
      <div class="phase-date">Days 61–90</div>
      <ul class="phase-items">
        <li>Design a system from scratch (whiteboard, no AI) for a hypothetical requirement</li>
        <li>Have AI critique your design — not design it for you</li>
        <li>Build context fluency: write a structured spec before asking AI to implement anything</li>
        <li>Practice code review: review AI-generated code with fresh eyes, find at least 2 issues</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>The Long Game</h3>
  <p>The developers who will thrive aren't the ones who avoid AI — they're the ones who develop deep expertise <em>and</em> leverage AI on top of it. The key insight from arXiv:2605.05400 is that context fluency scales with understanding: the more you know, the richer your specs, the better the AI output, the more leverage you get.</p>

  <p>Treat AI as a collaborator who's extremely fast but needs clear direction and supervision — not as an authority to defer to. Your job is to be the person who knows what to build, catches what the AI got wrong, and grows toward problems that require genuine judgment.</p>

  <div class="callout callout-green">
    <span class="callout-label">The meta-skill</span>
    The developers best positioned for the next decade aren't specialists in any single language or framework — those are increasingly commoditized. They're specialists in <em>problem decomposition, system design, and the judgment to direct AI effectively</em>. These compound. Start building them now.
  </div>

  <p>The productivity trap isn't AI itself — it's letting AI use become a substitution for thought rather than an amplification of it. With the right habits, you can have the speed <em>and</em> the growth. Sam's path is available to everyone starting today.</p>
</section>

